WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.180
Avengers: Infinity War had people crying in
their seats, but it could have also had audiences

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.800
barfing in their popcorn.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.540
In a recent conversation with IGN, co-screenwriter
Christopher Markus described a scene which

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.740
was scripted, but never shot, for the movie.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.140
It's a good thing, too, because it might have
taken the audience right out of the film's

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.620
story by making them sick to their stomachs.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:23.480
Markus described the scene, which would have
happened early on in Infinity War.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.320
He said,

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.920
"It was a scene of basically Widow and Falcon
and Cap in hiding post-Civil War, and they'd

00:00:28.929 --> 00:00:33.010
just been through a big fight with criminals,
and Cap was eating mashed potatoes, and Falcon

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.440
says 'You're bleeding into your mashed potatoes.'

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.620
[Cap] looks at [Falcon] like... yeah, he is
bleeding into his mashed potatoes.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.680
I remember Kevin Feige going 'He's bleeding
into his mashed potatoes?!

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.240
I don't want to see that!'

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.820
Yeah, maybe we went a little too... something."

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.220
"I think it's gratuitous, but whatever."

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.320
It's "far," Mr. Markus... the word you're
looking for is "far."

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.640
Stoic soldier that he is, we suppose it's
pretty easy to imagine Captain America being

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.429
wholly unconcerned by the fact that he's chowing
down on bloody potatoes but, as usual, it

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:07.610
appears that Feige's instincts were pretty
spot-on here, because we're sure that nobody

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:08.820
wanted to see that.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.200
Of course, when you're scripting an absolutely
massive crossover film like Infinity War,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.620
you're going to have a few things that don't
make the final cut.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:19.680
During their appearance at San Diego Comic-Con
this past summer, Markus and his co-writer

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.250
Stephen McFeely talked about a couple of other
notable scenes that didn't make it past the

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:27.560
conception stage one that would have been
really cool, and another that... well, would

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.040
have, quote, "sucked rocks," as McFeely delicately
put it.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:35.000
This would be the original version of Thor's
adventure, which in the finished film had

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.190
him journeying to Nidavellir to convince the
giant dwarf Eitri to craft him a battle axe

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.100
capable of taking down Thanos.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:46.020
It proved to be a tense, funny, memorable
sequence; Thor was forced to essentially kick-start

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:50.660
a dying star, he and Rocket began to forge
a sweet bond, and Groot assisted with mounting

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:54.560
Stormbreaker, the weapon with which Thor would
ultimately fail to go for the head.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.300
"You should've gone for the —"

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.400
Yeah, yeah, we know, Thanos.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:00.990
God, give it a rest already!

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:05.480
Anyway, it's not so much that Markus and McFeely's
conception of the sequence was different — it's

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.000
more that the pair simply had no idea what
to do with Thor during the film.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.560
Markus explained that what they came up with
was, quote, "much too adventure-related, and

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.940
insufficiently character-based," and involved
Thor battling a giant snake.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.120
McFeely admitted that while Infinity War's
script was in this draft, the duo actually

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.680
feared for their jobs:

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:24.640
He confessed:

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:27.980
"It was dark for a long time, because we hadn't
done well enough."

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:32.080
Eventually, the duo came up with the quest
to Nidavellir, which both made narrative sense

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.980
and provided some excellent character beats
for the God of Thunder.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.500
While it certainly sounds like their original
plans for Thor would have dragged the film

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.390
down in the middle, another scene that didn't
make it before the cameras one involving the

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:46.510
Sorcerer Supreme, Doctor Strange might have
been really cool, but would have also felt

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.650
ported over from a different movie entirely.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:53.230
The scene would have taken place during the
confrontation on Titan between Thanos, Strange,

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.980
Iron Man, Spider-Man, and the Guardians of
the Galaxy.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.380
During the fight, Strange would have employed
the same trick once used on him by the Ancient

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.500
One, giving the Mad Titan a poke in the head
that would have sent him hurtling through

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.540
the multiverse.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.940
Thanos would have been forced to relive all
of his genocidal crimes, and in the climactic

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.920
moment, we would have gotten our onscreen
introduction to an iconic and immensely powerful

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:18.000
Marvel character: the Living Tribunal, a godlike
being who ensures that balance is maintained

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.280
throughout the multiverse.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.780
The Tribunal would have judged Evil Grimace
harshly, but then, Thanos realizing that the

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:28.090
whole ordeal was just more "smoke and mirrors"
from Strange would have basically snapped

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.580
out of it, and returned to the fight against
our heroes.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:34.500
But while that sounds cool on paper, Markus
explained that the sequence still would have

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:35.940
thrown off the movie's pacing.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:36.680
He quipped:

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:40.850
"We were in the middle of a really fast-paced
fight, so to stop it for an LSD trip kind

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:42.380
of sucked the air out of it."

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.960
It's always interesting to know what might
have been, although it's safe to say that

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:50.710
Infinity War did not suffer for its lack of
giant serpents and bloody mashed potatoes.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:54.630
It just goes to show that even the creative
minds behind our favorite movies can stumble

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:59.030
pretty hard and it also makes us thankful
that Feige was around to remind the ace scribes

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.600
that they were not, in fact, writing a horror
movie.

